WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
Observability turns OpenRouter from a black box into an operable dependency.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:20.760
At minimum, record which model was requested, which provider served the request when that information is available, latency, token usage, status codes, fallback events, retry counts, and application feature names.

3
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:28.860
These signals help engineers distinguish model quality issues from routing issues, provider outages, prompt regressions, and cost changes.

4
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.020
Evaluation should happen before and after launch.

5
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:37.320
Before launch, build a representative set of prompts and expected qualities for the task.

6
00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.620
That set does not need to be enormous to be useful.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:50.240
It should include normal inputs, edge cases, ambiguous requests, long context cases, and examples where the system should refuse or ask for clarification.

8
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:56.340
Run the set when changing models, prompts, provider preferences, or decoding settings.

9
00:00:56.620 --> 00:01:00.940
After launch, watch trends rather than isolated anecdotes.

10
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:09.600
Track latency percentiles, error rates, user correction rates, fallback frequency, token consumption, and quality review samples.

11
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.020
A cheaper route that doubles corrections may not be cheaper in practice.

12
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:19.080
A faster model that fails on longer context may be a poor fit for a document workflow.

13
00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:26.700
Operational controls should connect cost, reliability, and quality instead of optimizing one metric alone.

14
00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.620
Finally, define change management.

15
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.160
Who can add a model?

16
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:32.940
Who can allow a new provider?

17
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.080
What review is required for sensitive data classes?

18
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.300
How quickly can the team disable a failing route?

19
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:47.240
These questions sound administrative, but they are practical safeguards for any LLM system that may change underneath active users.

20
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:54.380
When a route changes, the team should know whether it changed quality, privacy, latency, or cost.

21
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.920
Rollback criteria should be written before an incident starts.
